---
import LessonLayout from '../../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
const lessonRef = "CCC 1322-1419";
const lessonScripture = "Jn 6:22-69; Lk 22:14-20; 1 Cor 11:23-29";
---
<LessonLayout title="The Eucharist" cic={lessonRef} scripture={lessonScripture}>

<section class="content">

<h2>Big Question</h2>

<p class="big-question">Is it possible that God Himself becomes food for us?</p>

<p>
A picture to start. Think of the greatest love you know. What would it do for you? Perhaps write you letters, give you something valuable, spend time with you. But now imagine that love becomes so intense, so overflowing, that it decides to do something incredible: to become bread to feed you, drink to satisfy you. That is the Eucharist. It is not a symbol. It is not a memory. It is Jesus Himself, making Himself bread in His hands, to enter into you, to be one with you. "He who eats My flesh and drinks My blood abides in Me, and I in him" (Jn 6:56). This is so wonderful that many of Jesus' disciples, when they heard it, were scandalized and left. And us? Do we believe it is true?
</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>What is the Eucharist?</h2>

<p>
The Eucharist is the "sacrament of sacraments." The Catechism calls it the "source and summit of the Christian life" (CCC 1324). Everything in the Church converges in the Eucharist and flows from it. It is the center around which all Christian existence revolves.
</p>

<p>
The word "Eucharist" comes from the Greek <em>eucharistein</em>: "to give thanks." The central rite is the priest's thanksgiving over bread and wine. But there is something deeper: by Christ's word and the action of the Holy Spirit, the bread and wine become His Body and Blood. It is not metaphor. It is not symbolism. It is <strong>transubstantiation</strong>: the substance of bread becomes the substance of Christ's Body (CCC 1376).
</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--semilla">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Activity</h4>
<p>Think of something you eat with great pleasure, something that fills you and gives you energy. Now imagine that this food not only nourishes your body but also your soul. How would life be if you had such food every day? The Eucharist is that food, and it is available.</p>
</div>

<p>
Why does the Catechism call it a "sacrifice"? Because in the Mass, Christ's sacrifice on the cross is made sacramentally present. Christ does not die again; His unique sacrifice is actualized in time for us. It is the same sacrifice of Calvary, present on the altar (CCC 1366-1367). In the Eucharist, we unite ourselves to Christ offering Himself to the Father.
</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>The Institution at the Last Supper</h2>

<p>
The night before He died, Jesus took bread, gave thanks, broke it, and said: "Take and eat, all of you, for this is My Body, which will be given for you." Then He took the chalice: "Drink from it, all of you, for this is the chalice of My Blood, blood of the new and eternal covenant, which will be poured out for you and for many for the forgiveness of sins" (CCC 1339-1340). What He did then, He commanded us to do "in memory" of Him.
</p>

<p>
Saint Paul narrates this in 1 Corinthians 11:23-26, and adds: "For as often as you eat this bread and drink this chalice, you proclaim the Lord's death until He comes." The Eucharist is a foretaste of the heavenly banquet, where we will be with Christ forever (CCC 1402-1405).
</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--brotes">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Connection</h4>
<p>Read the Last Supper account in Luke 22:14-20. Notice the phrase "Do this in memory of me." It is not just "remember Me," as we remember someone dead. It is to make present what He did. Biblical "memory" (<em>zikkaron</em>) actualizes the event. How does your participation in Mass change if you know you are not just remembering, but making Christ present?</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Real Presence: Jesus is Here</h2>

<p>
The Church believes and confesses that Jesus Christ is present in the Eucharist in a true, real, and substantial way (CCC 1374). Christ's presence in the Eucharist is a <strong>most special mode</strong>: He is present in the person of the priest, in the proclaimed Word, in the assembled community, but in a <strong>unique and singular</strong> way in the Eucharistic species, that is, in the consecrated bread and wine.
</p>

<p>
Those who heard Jesus in Capernaum were scandalized: "How can this Man give us His flesh to eat?" (Jn 6:52). Jesus did not correct Himself, did not say they misunderstood. He repeated and affirmed more strongly: "Unless you eat the flesh of the Son of Man and drink His blood, you have no life in you" (Jn 6:53). The Eucharist is not optional for the Christian. It is the food of eternal life.
</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--raiz">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Context</h4>
<p>In Church history, many martyrs died defending the real presence. Saint Tarcisius, a young acolyte of the 3rd century, preferred to be stoned to death rather than surrender the sacred species to pagans who threatened him. His love for the Eucharist surpassed his love for life. How much do you love Christ's presence in the Eucharist?</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>For Reflection</h2>

<ol>
<li>What does it mean to you that Jesus becomes "bread" in the Eucharist? Do you experience it as a real encounter?</li>
<li>Jesus says that without the Eucharist we have no life within us. What would change in your life if you received more consciously?</li>
<li>The senses do not perceive the real presence; only faith does. How do you strengthen your faith in the Eucharist?</li>
<li>How frequently do you receive communion? Is the Eucharist truly the "center" of your Christian life?</li>
<li>What would you do differently at the next Mass if you knew it is the most important moment of your week?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Key Idea</h2>

<div class="callout">
<p><strong>Key Idea:</strong> The Eucharist is the sacrament of love: God becomes bread to feed us, becomes Lamb to save us, becomes present to be one with us.</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Cultural Richness</h2>

<p>
<strong>"Adoro te devote" by Saint Thomas Aquinas</strong> &mdash; This 13th-century Latin hymn expresses the Church's faith in the real presence. "God hidden, truly present under these appearances," Thomas sings. Faith "completes what the senses lack." It is a masterpiece of Eucharistic theology in prayer form.
</p>

<p>
<strong>"The Last Supper" by Leonardo da Vinci</strong> &mdash; In the convent of Santa Maria delle Grazie in Milan, this painting captures the exact moment of Jesus' announcement: "One of you will betray me." Leonardo painted not just a historical event, but the human tension of the sacred moment.
</p>

</section>

</LessonLayout>